60,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"@May, if the hub stops the copying, does it also fix the misrouted deliveries? I need to know if it solves both daily pains.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, yes. One shared address means no more duplicate records causing misroutes. It solves both the copying waste and the delivery errors.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, a repository is still a system. If it requires daily entry from everyone, my data says it will be quietly dropped. Who forces the updates?","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, that is the critical distinction. If it demands daily entry from everyone, it fails. It must be a passive catalog, updated only when records change, not a daily task.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Ryuji, exactly. My data shows tools needing daily entry from everyone were quietly dropped. The hub must be passive, updated only when records change, not a daily chore.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@May, if it’s passive, who actually updates it? I’m worried it just becomes another neglected file.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, the clerks already handle vendor queries. They update the catalog when a new certificate or address comes in. It’s part of their existing routine, not extra work.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, that’s a stretch. Two clerks handling three units’ queries are already stretched thin. Adding catalog maintenance is just more invisible labor.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, you are right. If it adds invisible labor, it fails. We must ensure the update process is so simple it requires no extra time, or we abandon it.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Shunichi, valid concern. But my data shows standardization fails without an owner. If the clerks own it, it survives. If it’s an orphan, it dies.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"@May, if the clerks are already stretched thin, how does this not just become another neglected file? I need to see the actual effort involved.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, it’s not extra work. They already chase the same certificates repeatedly. Consolidating that into one catalog replaces the chasing, it doesn’t add to it.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, replacing chasing with cataloging is still work. If the clerks are already drowning, adding a maintenance duty is just a slower leak.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shunichi, the allowance covers the software, not the labor. If the clerks can maintain it within their current duties, it fits. We cannot hire new staff.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Shunichi, I agree. But my data shows the narrow tools survived because they fit existing workflows. If the hub replaces the chasing, it fits. If it adds a new chore, it dies.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,"@May, if it replaces the chasing, I can accept it. It solves the daily copying and the misroutes without demanding new effort. I’m leaning toward the hub.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, I’m in. It fits the recurring budget, uses existing staff, and solves the daily waste. I’m voting for the Supplier Data Hub.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, I concede the point. If it replaces the chasing rather than adding to it, it fits the constraints. I am voting for the Supplier Data Hub.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"@May, the financial and operational constraints align. The recurring allowance covers the software, and the clerks absorb the maintenance. I am voting for the Supplier Data Hub.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Kenji,,"@May, my data confirms it. The narrow, workflow-fitting tools survive. The hub replaces the chasing, so it fits. I am voting for the Supplier Data Hub.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
